        <p class="lead text-xl text-gray-700 font-medium">
          Most executive teams feel like they're steering execution because they meet often. That feeling is exactly the problem: meeting frequency has never guaranteed steering quality — and the time meetings absorb doesn't go anywhere else.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-teal rounded-r-xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-2">Sources cited</p>
          <p class="text-gray-700">Leslie A. Perlow, Constance Noonan Hadley &amp; Eunice Eun, "Stop the Meeting Madness," Harvard Business Review, 2017; Steven G. Rogelberg, <em>The Surprising Science of Meetings</em>, Oxford University Press, 2019; Michael Mankins, Chris Brahm &amp; Gregory Caimi, "Your Scarcest Resource," Harvard Business Review, 2014 (Bain &amp; Company).</p>
        </div>

        <h2>Meeting time has exploded</h2>
        <p>
          Research widely cited by Harvard Business Review shows that today's executives spend an average of <strong>23 hours a week in meetings</strong> — up from less than 10 hours in the 1960s. Meeting time has more than doubled in sixty years, without steering quality improving proportionally.
        </p>
        <p>
          Bain &amp; Company documented what has become a classic example: a single three-hour weekly Executive Committee meeting, once cascaded into preparation and follow-up meetings at every level of the organisation, can mobilise <strong>up to 300,000 hours of work a year</strong>. This isn't an isolated case — it's a structural cascade effect present in most large organisations as soon as a meeting ritual goes unquestioned.
        </p>
        <p>The direct consequence: the majority of executives surveyed in these same studies consider their steering meetings unproductive or inefficient. The problem isn't just volume — it's the gap between time invested and value actually produced.</p>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-teal rounded-r-xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-2">A number that reframes the debate</p>
          <p class="text-gray-700">300,000 hours a year isn't the cost of one bad meeting — it's the cost of a ritual that was never questioned, which simply became the default way of "doing steering."</p>
        </div>

        <h2>Being in a meeting isn't steering</h2>
        <p>
          A meeting gives the impression of controlling execution: indicators get presented, blockers get discussed, everyone leaves with minutes. But this ritual reassures more than it steers. Real steering — checking that decisions actually produce the expected results — mostly happens <em>between</em> meetings, not during them.
        </p>
        <p>
          It's the same gap described in our article on <a href="/en/resources/who-really-has-the-right-to-decide">decision rights</a>: a well-run ritual (the meeting itself) guarantees nothing about what happens once everyone leaves the room. A strategy committee, a weekly check-in, a project steering committee, a follow-up meeting: each produces minutes that get circulated — and that's often where real follow-through stops.
        </p>

        <h2>4 signals that betray false steering</h2>

        <h3>1. Minutes replace follow-through</h3>
        <p>The meeting ends, and no one checks that the decided actions were actually completed before the next meeting.</p>

        <h3>2. The same topics keep coming back</h3>
        <p>A point gets raised meeting after meeting, without ever really being settled — a sign the meeting is for discussing, not deciding.</p>

        <h3>3. No one knows who's supposed to decide</h3>
        <p>The meeting is used to "align" rather than to decide: the decision dissolves into the group, and no one is individually accountable for the outcome.</p>

        <h3>4. Steering only exists in the meeting room</h3>
        <p>Between two committees, no one actually looks at the indicators — the dashboard only gets consulted when it's projected on screen.</p>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-teal rounded-r-xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-2">The hidden cost</p>
          <p class="text-gray-700">Steering that only exists in meetings doesn't just cost meeting time — it costs the decisions that never get followed through between committees, while everyone believes things are "moving forward."</p>
        </div>

        <h2>The question executive committees should be asking</h2>
        <p>Not just: "Do we have a follow-up meeting on this?"</p>
        <p>But rather: <strong>"If we cut half our follow-up meetings, would steering actually get worse?"</strong></p>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-teal rounded-r-xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-1">Diagnostic question</p>
          <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm">On your last steering committee: how many decisions made were actually followed by a verified action the week after?</p>
        </div>

